>> title: Attar
>> category: Sauces & Gravies
>> tags: sugar syrup, middle eastern, orange blossom, pastry, component
>> counters: Shawarma Counter
>> aka: attar, ater, atar, qatr, qater, sugar syrup, simple syrup, sherbet, baklava syrup, rose syrup
>> pairs-with: pita-bread
>> servings: 16
>> time: 2 hr 20 min
>> step.1: dissolve the sugar over low heat
>> step.2: simmer 10 min to a light thread
>> step.3: stir in the lemon
>> step.4: perfume off the heat
>> step.5: cool 2 hr, then chill

Dissolve @granulated sugar{2%cups}(400 g) in @water{1%cup}(240 mL) in a #saucepan{} over low heat, stirring only until the last grain has gone and not once after. Stirring a boiling syrup is how you get a pan of crystals.

Simmer @&(~1)syrup{} without stirring ~simmer{10%min}, until a drop pulled between finger and thumb draws a short thread. Any longer and it sets hard in the tray instead of soaking in.

Stir @lemon juice{1%Tbs}(15 mL) into @&(~1)hot syrup{}. The acid keeps it pourable for weeks in the fridge instead of going grainy in three days.

Take @&(~1)syrup{} off the heat and stir in @orange blossom water{1%Tbs}(15 mL) and @rose water{1%tsp}(5 mL). These go in off the heat every time — both are volatile, and a syrup perfumed while it is still boiling smells of nothing by the time it is cold.

Cool @&(~1)attar{} ~cool{2%hr}, then chill it. The rule everything it touches obeys is that the two must be at opposite temperatures: cold syrup onto a hot tray of baklava, or hot syrup over cold cake. Hot on hot goes soggy, cold on cold sits on the surface.
